%% args
--help

%% stdout
envctl — manage keys in env files

Commands:
  envctl set     [file] <KEY> [VALUE]   set/replace KEY (uncomments if commented)
  envctl get     [file] <KEY>           print active value; exit 1 if unset
  envctl disable [file] <KEY>           comment KEY out, keep its value
  envctl enable  [file] <KEY>           uncomment KEY
  envctl delete  [file] <KEY>           remove KEY entirely (active + commented) [rm]
  envctl list    [file] [--values] [--all]  active keys; --values shows values;
                                        --all also lists disabled keys           [ls]
  envctl redact  [file]                 filter stdin to stdout, masking secrets

File: optional when ./.env exists as a regular file. If the first positional is
an existing regular file, it is used; otherwise .env is assumed when present.

Bare form (no command word):
  envctl [file] <KEY>            == get
  envctl [file] <KEY> <VALUE>    == set

Flags:
  --dry-run   mutating command: print a unified diff, write nothing
  --values    list: show values (secret-looking ones follow redact rules)
  --all       list: include disabled keys tagged (disabled)
  --no-env    redact: skip the env file's literal values, use heuristics only
              (redact takes a file or --no-env, never both)
  --redact    mask secret-looking values on get / list --values / dry-run
  --raw       never mask (overrides auto-redact and --redact; redact rejects it)

Redaction (presentation only; pipes and scripts stay raw so get stays composable):
  mask as <redacted> / <redacted:private-key> / <redacted:credentials> when on.
  Signals: key-name segments (case-insensitive), PEM private keys, PuTTY keys,
  private JWKs, credentialed URLs, sensitive URL query parameters, Bearer and
  Basic values, connection-string password fragments, known token prefixes,
  JWTs, and Shannon entropy under a suspicious key name. Path-like key suffixes
  (*_FILE/*_PATH/*_NAME) and digest-like key names (*_SHA/*_HASH/*_COMMIT) mask
  only when the value itself looks secret; *_ID keys skip the entropy bar but
  still mask under a strong secret name. A multiline quoted or PEM value masks
  to one line and its continuation lines are never printed.
Default on when a coding agent is detected and stdout is a TTY; off when
stdout is piped/redirected unless --redact. --raw always shows full secrets.

Filter mode: envctl redact reads stdin and writes stdout with secrets masked.
  It always redacts and rejects --raw. Every maskable value in the env file is
  matched literally, together with its base64, URL-encoded and JSON-escaped
  forms; value-shape heuristics then run over the rest of the text. Entropy
  applies only where a key name is present. A PEM private key prints as one
  <redacted:private-key> line and its body is dropped, up to 512 lines when
  the END marker never arrives.

Guarantees: only the target key's logical assignment changes (a multiline value
moves with its continuation lines); re-running with the same args is a no-op;
writes are atomic (temp + rename) and preserve file mode; VALUE is literal (no
shell/regex reinterpretation).
